{
  "schema": "ora.toolkit-tool.v1",
  "name": "grasp_plan",
  "description": "Generate inspectable public RGB-D grasp candidates for one current target.\nUse when: current grounding or segmentation identifies the intended object and you need parallel-jaw poses. Select the arm on dual-arm embodiments.\nEffect: read-only; queries Contact-GraspNet using the selected source-camera RGB-D and compiles poses to the ARX X5 link6 controlled point; the embodiment card gives its fixed offset to the jaw contact centre. Top-down candidates from the target mask (jaw centre at a support-aware mid-height) join the learned ones. Optional synchronized views provide review evidence.\nReturns: up to three candidates, complete world-frame pregrasp_pose and contact_pose, each with approach_world and jaw_line_world, the world directions of its approach axis and jaw line (move_pose's world_axes fields), copyable bounded contact_approach targets, predicted opening, compiler checks and labeled review images. gripper_opening_m is the learned width prediction or, top-down, the mask width plus clearance, admitted up to the 80 mm nominal ceiling; aperture_calibration states that it is not measured pad clearance. Reachability here is the planning-time IK reference described under IK selection below; no plan_ref is returned. Inspect every candidate panel and choose target identity and geometry independently of rank. After choosing a current candidate, use grasp_execute(candidate_id, rationale) when you accept its committed open/pregrasp/approach/close/lift/verify sequence without intermediate decisions. Choose manual move_pose plus set_gripper when you need a separate observation or decision before closing. Choose this path before moving: any world action stales the candidate, so call grasp_plan again before later using grasp_execute. Pose handoff: when manually commanding a returned world pose, set position_mode=absolute and orientation_mode=absolute_quaternion, then copy position_m and orientation_xyzw unchanged. Put these fields under targets.<selected_arm>. Choose interaction by the intended physical contact. Schema example only; replace the illustrative pose and arm with your selected current result:\n{\"tool\":\"move_pose\",\"arguments\":{\"targets\":{\"right\":{\"position_mode\":\"absolute\",\"position_m\":[0.1,0.0,0.9],\"orientation_mode\":\"absolute_quaternion\",\"orientation_xyzw\":[0.0,0.0,0.0,1.0],\"interaction\":\"precision\"}}}}\nDoes not: plan trajectories, certify reachability, move the robot, command a gripper, use object ground truth, certify retention or report task success. Backend rank is not a success probability. Missing optional review cameras do not invalidate usable primary input.\nOn error: Refused: a target_ref unknown in this round (target_ref_unavailable) or bound to an earlier observation (stale_observation); max_candidates outside 1 to 3; an arm outside this tool's enum; unusable primary RGB-D or a transient backend failure (grasp_proposal_failed); a request the grasp-proposal service rejects (invalid_arguments, with the service's detail). Not refused but moved to diagnostics.rejected: a candidate whose predicted opening exceeds the nominal ceiling above, or whose controlled point or pregrasp lies outside the action workspace; with none left the result is status no_candidate. Obtain a better current view or select another target or candidate. Recompute after the target or observation changes. After a submitted motion fails, use its phase and measured residual to choose the next action. Retention evidence remains advisory. Prepared grasp selection: grasp_execute opens first and releases anything held, then uses the candidate pregrasp and contact poses and a 3 cm world-up probe. Select it only when opening and lifting are appropriate; otherwise use manual motion. Candidates are geometry proposals, not reachability certificates. For grasp_execute, the selected pregrasp and contact poses receive one backend check before opening. Manual pose submissions use normal backend admission. IK selection: before the max_candidates display cap, every compiled candidate in a pool of up to 32 is checked with this backend's read-only inverse kinematics -- pregrasp_pose as a free_space move from the current joints, then contact_pose as a contact_approach chained from that pregrasp, trying the compiled orientation and then its 180-degree jaw flip. Candidates it calls reachable are shown first, those whose approach is within 30 degrees of straight down before the rest, each group in backend order; the cap then keeps the first max_candidates and nothing else is dropped. Each shown candidate carries ik_reachability {reachable, member, pregrasp, approach, approach_tilt_from_vertical_deg}, and the result's ik_reachability states the solver, how many pool candidates were checked and how many were reachable. It is an IK reference at planning time, not a guarantee: every motion still runs its own backend admission, and matches_move_pose_admission says whether this solver is that one. reachable null means the check was unavailable and the backend order was kept. Jaw symmetry: on this arm the same grasp turned 180 degrees about the approach axis differs only in joint6, the wrist roll (limit +/-179.91 deg). Both turns are checked, and pregrasp_pose and contact_pose are published in the reachable turn with more joint6 margin, then the one needing the smaller joint move, then the compiled one. ik_reachability.jaw_symmetry names the published turn (exact or jaw_flip), why, and each turn's joint6 margin; ik_reachability.joint_headroom gives its smallest joint-limit and joint6 margins at the pregrasp and contact solutions. Little joint6 margin leaves little wrist rotation after the grasp. Planning-time IK numbers, not a carry guarantee. Fixed texts stated once here and left out of results and effects: ik_reachability.claim_boundary is \"IK reference at planning time for the compiled pregrasp (free_space from the measured joints) and approach (contact_approach from that pregrasp solution), trying the exact orientation and its 180-degree jaw flip; not a guarantee. Execution runs its own backend check, and any world action stales this.\"; ik_reachability.jaw_symmetry.claim_boundary is \"Joint-limit margins of the backend's read-only IK solutions for the published pregrasp and contact poses at planning time; joint6 is the X5 wrist roll about the approach axis and is reported only when the measured joints prove it is the binding joint. An IK reference, not a carry guarantee: execution solves again, and lifting, carrying or rotating the wrist after the grasp moves every margin.\".",
  "inputSchema": {
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
      "arm": {
        "description": "Arm whose wrist RGB-D joins head in scene fusion and whose source-bound ARX X5 end-effector compiler is used.",
        "enum": [
          "left",
          "right"
        ],
        "type": "string"
      },
      "max_candidates": {
        "default": 3,
        "description": "How many candidates to return, 1 to 3, default 3: the front of the IK-ordered pool described under IK selection. Null means omitted.",
        "maximum": 3,
        "minimum": 1,
        "type": [
          "integer",
          "null"
        ]
      },
      "target_ref": {
        "description": "Current observation-bound target from grounding or segmentation; target-mask points remain tied to its source camera.",
        "pattern": "^target-[0-9a-f]{24}$",
        "type": "string"
      }
    },
    "required": [
      "arm",
      "target_ref"
    ],
    "type": "object"
  },
